From bbb5b9706fdd1c15fd990f3783352870337a789f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 00:31:18 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 08/15] avd-fw: queue ABI 2, SRAM ring programs and multi-kick
 PP

PIODMA program fetches need the decoder's second DART stream, which the
device trees in use do not describe. Add a mode that needs no DMA: the host
streams each program into a 0x1800-word ring in CM3 SRAM (+0x0, unused in
queue mode) and publishes a free-running write count; firmware copies up
to 64 words per step into the VP FIFO, publishes its read count, and
faults if a descriptor's start disagrees with the ring position.

Descriptors gain flags (bit 0: program in the SRAM ring), pp_extra and
pp_cont, so HEVC, VP9 and AV1 frames that need one PP EXEC per segment
get the same kick sequence the legacy host wrote. A PP DONE while no frame
is in PP is counted and ignored instead of halting the queue, matching
the legacy host. The Tansy VP0 counter in SRAM is only updated in legacy
mode now, so it cannot land inside the ring.

The info block reports ABI 2, the feature bits and the ring geometry.
Host unit tests and the unicorn transport test cover wrap, streaming
past the ring size, FIFO back-pressure, multi-kick, stray PP DONE and the
position check on all seven images.
